DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260403
SUMMARY:Lectio Terra: Dutch Crocus
DESCRIPTION:With Guillaume Barth\, William Herbert.\n\nThe crocuses likely 
 have already come and gone. They can return each spring almost too early\,
  before the snow melts\, before the robins return\, before the atmosphere 
 warms (let alone the soil). Here in my urban neighborhood\, the Dutch croc
 us (Crocus vernus) blooms in tandem with the snowdrops (Galanthus nivalis)
  and just prior to the daffodils (Narcissus pseudonarcissus). These corm-b
 ased geophytes\, which require 10 to 15 weeks of cold to biochemically shi
 ft their apical meristem\, or growing point\, from leaf- to flower-product
 ion\, have become such a seasonal marker for me that I assumed they…\n\n
 Read the essay: https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/dutch-crocus/
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/dutch-crocus/
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
