DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260501
SUMMARY:Lectio Terra: Sheet Moss
DESCRIPTION:With Hildegard von Bingen\, Michael Marder.\n\nIf there’s a p
 lant that reminds me of God\, it’s probably moss. Indeed\, the plant’s
  biology is as ornate and rarefied as any systematic theology. Ubiquitous 
 and abundant but amorphously changing\, obvious but mysterious\, perennial
 ly surprising and yet easy to take for granted\, expanding or contracting 
 as it slowly transforms its surroundings—modest moss can be as soft as t
 he tender\, mighty mullein\, but its diminutive\, hair-like roots\, called
  rhizoids\, work over time to accomplish feats of erosion that would be im
 possible for other plants. Moss is slow green fire\, breathing air\, water
 \, and…\n\nRead the essay: https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/sheet-m
 oss/
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/sheet-moss/
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
