DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
SUMMARY:Lectio Terra: Staghorn Sumac
DESCRIPTION:With Cole Swanson\, Katharine Lee Bates\, J. Francis Macbride.\
 n\nIs it a bush? Is it a tree? Is it a poison? Is it lemonade? In the lowe
 r gaps of the forest\, or in its damaged peripheries\, the sumac thrives. 
 Like the pawpaw\, it often spreads through root suckers\, producing a grov
 e of clones in meadow edges\, riverbanks\, or cleared land. Its conical fl
 owers and then spike-like berry clusters develop over the summer\, and its
  leaves erupt into vibrant colors in the early fall. The plant’s leaves 
 and bark contain tannins that were used for centuries\, around the world\,
  to tan light and flexible leathers and as a mordant to dye fabrics. In No
 rth America\, the…\n\nRead the essay: https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-te
 rra/staghorn-sumac/
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/staghorn-sumac/
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
