BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lt-0035-first@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261115
SUMMARY:Lectio Terra: Eastern Grey Squirrel
DESCRIPTION:With Meriwether Lewis\, John Burroughs\, Ralph Waldo Emerson.\n
 \nMy brother called them “joeys” and “jojos” as a child\, for reas
 ons that escape me. Maybe he was thinking of young marsupials or mispronou
 ncing the word “chipmunk.” At any rate\, squirrels were one of the fir
 st forms of wildlife detectable through our family’s windows\, in our ba
 ckyard\, or at the playgrounds we frequented. Like many suburban household
 s\, our closest neighbors were likely not people but squirrels\, who raise
  their young in tree hollows or in leafy nests called dreys\, breeding twi
 ce annually: in late winter and again in summer. Indeed\, these quintessen
 tial critters have a persistent…\n\nRead the essay: https://earthlyhours
 .com/lectio-terra/eastern-grey-squirrel/
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/eastern-grey-squirrel/
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
