BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lt-0036-first@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261122
SUMMARY:Lectio Terra: Fen and Wetland
DESCRIPTION:With Charles Kingsley\, Amy Lowell\, Arielle Kaplan\, John Clar
 e.\n\nWhile fall may be the season of the woodlands\, the transition into 
 winter can shift plant and animal activity toward the lower\, tighter stru
 cture of all those water-adjacent landforms: fens\, marshlands\, bogs\, mi
 res and quagmires\, swamps\, sloughs\, and swales—not to mention bayous\
 , pocosins\, and the many other regional variations of wetlands. If you’
 ve never stopped to wonder how these terms relate and differ\, you’re no
 t alone. Here’s what I’ve learned through a couple late nights spent d
 own the (water-logged) rabbit hole.\n\nRead the essay: https://earthlyhour
 s.com/lectio-terra/fen-and-wetland/
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/lectio-terra/fen-and-wetland/
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lt-0036@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20271122
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Lectio Terra: Fen and Wetland
DESCRIPTION:With Charles Kingsley\, Amy Lowell\, Arielle Kaplan\, John Clar
 e.\n\nWhile fall may be the season of the woodlands\, the transition into 
 winter can shift plant and animal activity toward the lower\, tighter stru
 cture of all those water-adjacent landforms: fens\, marshlands\, bogs\, mi
 res and quagmires\, swamps\, sloughs\, and swales—not to mention bayous\
 , pocosins\, and the many other regional variations of wetlands. If you’
 ve never stopped to wonder how these terms relate and differ\, you’re no
