BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0023@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20271123
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:American Chestnut
DESCRIPTION:NOW-A-DAYS one often hears the question\, “What is going to b
 ecome of our chestnut trees?” In fact\, whenever the subject of trees is
  broached in the course of a conversation\, this inquiry is bound to come 
 out—not\, as I believe\, that the interrogator hopes to receive a satisf
 actory answer\, but more in the way of a general query.\n— Arthur H. Gra
 ves\, “The Future of the Chestnut Tree in North America”\, Popular Sci
 ence Monthly 84 (June 1914)\, p. 551\n\nToday’s image: Mary Vaux Walcott
 \, American Chestnut (Castanea dentata) (1932)\, watercolour\n\nThe day in
  The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-2
 3
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-23
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0012@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20271124
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Hubbard Squash
DESCRIPTION:In handling the squashes\, I need hardly reiterate the caution 
 of care. My practice is to form a string of boys\, from the wagon to the s
 helves\, and the squashes are tossed from one to another\, with the cautio
 n to handle them like eggs. Boys well trained will not drop more than one 
 squash to the ton\, and I have known my boys to pass several tons without 
 dropping a single squash.\n\nIf the squash-house has been built with refer
 ence to warmth\, when once filled with squashes\, it is surprising with wh
 at little fire frost can be kept out. The mass of squashes are\, in themse
 lves\, a great store-house of heat\, and with inside shutters for the cold
 est weather\, the building is frost proof\, with a small outlay of fuel.\n
 — James J. H. Gregory\, “The Storing of the Crop\,” from Squashes: H
 ow to Grow Them (1867)\, pp. 35–36\n\nToday’s image: James J. H. Grego
 ry\, “Section of Squash-House\,” from Squashes: How to Grow Them (1867
 )\, p. 34\, wood engraving\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https:
 //earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-24
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-24
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0013@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20271125
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:The Three Sisters
DESCRIPTION:The corn was carefully dropped in the hills so as not to break 
 the germs which had nearly burst through. Among the Senecas\, in planting 
 corn the seeds of the squash and bean were sown in every seventh hill beca
 use it was thought that the spirits of these three plants were inseparable
 . They were called Dioʹhe″ko\, these sustain us. In the Green Corn Than
 ksgiving the leader rises and says\, “Diettinoⁿʹñioʻ diohe″koⁿ\
 , we give thanks to our sustainers.”\n\nCertain women banded themselves 
 together in a society called the Toñwisas or Toⁿwiʹsas Oäʹno. They p
 ropitiated the spirits of the three sisters by certain ceremonies. In thei
 r ceremonial march\, Wenŭntoñwiʹsăs\, the leader holding an armful of 
 corn and a cake of corn bread leads her band in a measured march about a k
 ettle of corn soup.\n— Arthur C. Parker\, from Iroquois Uses of Maize an
 d Other Food Plants (1910)\, p. 27\n\nToday’s image: A Seneca youth\, th
 e march of the Toñwiʹsas\, the women’s society of the three sisters\, 
 pen drawing printed as figure 2 in Arthur C. Parker\, Iroquois Uses of Mai
 ze and Other Food Plants (1910)\, p. 28 (printed sideways on the page)\n\n
 The day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.htm
 l?day=11-25
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-25
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0001@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261126
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Wild Turkey
DESCRIPTION:I am\, on this account\, not displeas’d that the Figure is no
 t known as a Bald Eagle\, but looks more like a Turk’y. For in Truth\, t
 he Turk’y is in comparison a much more respectable Bird\, and withal a t
 rue original Native of America. Eagles have been found in all Countries\, 
 but the Turk’y was peculiar to ours\; the first of the Species seen in E
 urope being brought to France by the Jesuits from Canada\, and serv’d up
  at the Wedding Table of Charles the Ninth. He is\, [though a little vain 
 and silly\, it is true\, but not the worse emblem for that\,] a Bird of Co
 urage\, and would not hesitate to attack a Grenadier of the British Guards
 \, who should presume to invade his Farm Yard with a red Coat on.\n— Ben
 jamin Franklin\, from a letter to Sarah Bache (Passy\, 26 January 1784)\n\
 nToday’s image: James McNeill Whistler\, Turkeys\, a Venice courtyard et
 ched in 1880 and published in the “Second Venice Set” (1886). When the
  edition was complete\, the copper was scored with crossed lines so no mor
 e could be printed\, and this proof was pulled to show that it was finishe
 d.\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seaso
 ns.html?day=11-26
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-26
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0002@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261127
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:American Cranberry
DESCRIPTION:Wuchipoquámeneash [Narragansett: berries of a sour kind]. A ki
 nd of sharp Fruit like a Barbary in tast.\n\nSasémineash [Narragansett: 
 “very sour berries\,” the cranberry] another sharp cooling Fruit growi
 ng in fresh Waters all the Winter\, Excellent in conserve against Feavers.
 \n— Roger Williams\, from A Key into the Language of America (1643)\, p.
  99\; the glosses in brackets follow J. Hammond Trumbull’s notes to the 
 1866 edition\n\nToday’s image: Lewis Wickes Hine\, Merilda. Carrying cra
 nberries\, Eldridge Bog\, near Rochester\, Massachusetts (September 1911)\
 , photograph for the National Child Labor Committee\n\nThe day in The Eart
 hly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-27
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-27
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0003@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261128
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Winterberry
DESCRIPTION:The prinos berries on their light-brown twigs are quite abundan
 t and handsome.\n\nWhile most keep close to their parlor fires this cold a
 nd blustering Thanksgiving afternoon\, and think with compassion of those 
 who are abroad\, I find the sunny south side of this swamp as warm as thei
 r parlors\, and warmer to my spirit. Aye\, there is a serenity and warmth 
 here which the parlor does not suggest\, enhanced by the sound of the wind
  roaring on the northwest side of the swamp a dozen or so rods off. What a
  wholesome and inspiring warmth is this!\n— Henry David Thoreau\, from h
 is journal (25 November 1858)\n\nToday’s image: D. W. Moody\, Prinos ver
 ticillatus (Winter Berry\; now Ilex verticillata)\, plate 10 of Asa B. Str
 ong\, The American Flora\, vol. 4 (1855)\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of
  Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-28
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-28
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0024@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261130
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:American Beaver
DESCRIPTION:In preparing for the winter\, their greatest efforts in tree cu
 tting are made. They commence in the latter part of September\, and contin
 ue through October and into November the several employments of cutting an
 d storing their winter wood\, and of repairing their lodges and dams. Thes
 e months are the season of their active labors\, which are only arrested b
 y the early snows and the formation of ice in their ponds.\n— Lewis H. M
 organ\, The American Beaver and His Works (1868)\, p. 168\n\nToday’s ima
 ge: Allen & Ginter\, Beaver\, from the Quadrupeds series (N41)\, a cigaret
 te card (1890)\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhou
 rs.com/seasons.html?day=11-30
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=11-30
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0005@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Skim Ice
DESCRIPTION:We have thus made ourselves acquainted with the beautiful snow-
 flowers self-constructed by the molecules of water in calm cold air. Do th
 e molecules show this architectural power when ordinary water is frozen? W
 hat\, for example\, is the structure of the ice over which we skate in win
 ter? Quite as wonderful as the flowers of the snow. The observation is rar
 e\, if not new\, but I have seen in water slowly freezing six-rayed ice-st
 ars formed\, and floating free on the surface. A six-rayed star\, moreover
 \, is typical of the construction of all our lake ice. It is built up of s
 uch forms wonderfully interlaced.\n— John Tyndall\, from The Forms of Wa
 ter in Clouds and Rivers\, Ice and Glaciers (1872)\, § 96\, p. 35\n\nToda
 y’s image: Central News Photo Service\, A Life Saving Appliance to Prote
 ct Those Who Like to Skate on Thin Ice (1917)\, photograph: George Hanlon\
 , foreman of parks for the borough of Queens\, with his apparatus for reac
 hing skaters who have fallen through\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hou
 rs: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-01
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-01
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0006@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261202
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Fall Cankerworm
DESCRIPTION:It was formerly supposed that the canker-worm moths came out of
  the ground only in the spring. It is now known that many of them rise in 
 the autumn and in the early part of the winter. In mild and open winters I
  have seen them in every month from October to March. They begin to make t
 heir appearance after the first hard frosts in the autumn\, usually toward
 s the end of October\, and they continue to come forth\, in greater or sma
 ller numbers\, according to the mildness or severity of the weather after 
 the frosts have begun.\n— Thaddeus William Harris\, from A Treatise on S
 ome of the Insects Injurious to Vegetation (1862)\, p. 462\n\nToday’s im
 age: B. A. Porter and C. H. Alden\, Fall Cankerworm\, plate II of The Cank
 erworms (U.S. Department of Agriculture\, 1924)\n\nThe day in The Earthly 
 Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-02
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-02
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0020@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261203
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Winter Moth
DESCRIPTION:The female is greyish to greyish-brown in colour\, and differs 
 totally from the male\, having the merest traces of wings\, which are tota
 lly useless as organs of flight . . .\n\nThe males have been observed for 
 many years to appear some days before the females. They may be seen at dus
 k\, flying lazily along hedgerows and in the orchards and gardens. They ar
 e attracted by light. One often sees them collected on street lamps\, and 
 they occur outside windows.\n— Fred. V. Theobald\, “The Winter Moth\,
 ” from The Insect and Other Allied Pests of Orchard\, Bush and Hothouse 
 Fruits (1909)\, pp. 51–52\n\nToday’s image: F. Edenden\, “Females an
 d males of the winter moth\,” fig. 37 from Fred. V. Theobald\, The Insec
 t and Other Allied Pests of Orchard\, Bush and Hothouse Fruits (1909)\, p.
  51\, photograph\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyh
 ours.com/seasons.html?day=12-03
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-03
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0019@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261204
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Winter Spiders
DESCRIPTION:Bushes may be swept in the same way\, or may be shaken over an 
 open umbrella\, or a piece of cloth or paper. In winter\, when spiders are
  torpid\, great numbers can be found by sifting the dead leaves that have 
 been lying for some years in the woods. A common coal sieve is fine enough
  to hold the leaves while the spiders and sticks and dirt pass through\, a
 nd may be picked over on a cloth or carried home in a bag and examined in 
 the house. The sifting should be repeated several times\, as many of the s
 piders hold to threads among the leaves and become loosened only after muc
 h shaking.\n— J. H. Emerton\, from The Common Spiders of the United Stat
 es (1902)\, p. xviii\n\nToday’s image: J. H. Emerton\, “Web of Tetragn
 atha in tansy plants\, showing the spider in its usual position\,” fig. 
 459 from The Common Spiders of the United States (1902)\, p. 201\, photogr
 aph\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seas
 ons.html?day=12-04
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-04
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0009@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261205
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Princess Pine
DESCRIPTION:The Fern allies have also played their appointed part in the do
 mestic and decorative economy of this and other generations. The scouring-
 rushes served our ancestors for keeping white their floors and wooden-ware
  in the days when carpets were a luxury. The trailing stems of various spe
 cies of Lycopodium have long been valued for holiday decorations\; while t
 heir burning spores have flashed in triumphal processions\, and have added
  their glow to the fervor of political campaigns.\n— Lucien Marcus Under
 wood\, from Our Native Ferns and Their Allies (1881\; 4th ed. 1893)\, p. i
 x\n\nToday’s image: Julian Alden Weir\, Christmas Greens (1889)\, etchin
 g\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/season
 s.html?day=12-05
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-05
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0010@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261207
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Earliest Sunset
DESCRIPTION:The lakes of ice gleam bluer than the lakes\nOf water ’neath 
 the summer sunshine gleamed:\nFar fairer than when placidly it streamed\,\
 nThe brook its frozen architecture makes\,\nAnd under bridges white its sw
 ift way takes.\nSnow comes and goes as messenger who dreamed\nMight linger
  on the road\; or one who deemed\nHis message hostile gently for their sak
 es\nWho listened might reveal it by degrees.\nWe gird against the cold of 
 winter wind\nOur loins now with mighty bands of sleep\,\nIn longest\, dark
 est nights take rest and ease\,\nAnd every shortening day\, as shadows cre
 ep\nO’er the brief noontide\, fresh surprises find.\n— Helen Hunt Jack
 son\, “December\,” from A Calendar of Sonnets (1886)\n\nToday’s imag
 e: George Inness\, A Winter Sky (1866)\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of H
 ours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-07
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-07
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0014@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261208
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:White Oak
DESCRIPTION:In the fall they change to a bright violet colour\, and form an
  agreeable contrast with the surrounding foliage which has not yet suffere
 d by the frost.\n\nThis is the only Oak on which a few of the dried leaves
  persist till the circulation is renewed in the spring. By this peculiarit
 y and by the whiteness of the bark\, from which it derives its name\, it i
 s easily distinguished in the winter.\n— François André Michaux\, “W
 hite Oak\,” from The North American Sylva\, vol. 1 (1819)\, p. 19\n\nTod
 ay’s image: Pierre-Joseph Redouté (drawing) and Bessin (engraving)\, Wh
 ite Oak\, plate 1 from François André Michaux\, The North American Sylva
 \, vol. 1 (1819)\, colour-printed stipple engraving\n\nThe day in The Eart
 hly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-08
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-08
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0004@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261210
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Late Oyster
DESCRIPTION:The species is not noticeably viscid after its youth. The visci
 dity can be detected in old specimens by moistening the pileus. Its flavor
  is not marked\, nor is its texture as pleasing as most others of its genu
 s\, but being a late species it satisfies the longing of the mycophagist f
 or his accustomed food.\n— Charles McIlvaine\, from Toadstools\, Mushroo
 ms\, Fungi\, Edible and Poisonous (1900)\, p. 146\n\nToday’s image: M. C
 . Cooke\, Agaricus (Pleurotus) serotinus\, figure B of plate 258 of Illust
 rations of British Fungi (1881–1891)\, detail\n\nThe day in The Earthly 
 Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-10
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-10
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0015@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261214
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Geminids
DESCRIPTION:One can spend a profitable and pleasurable half-hour in explori
 ng the wonders of Gemini. The whole constellation\, from head to foot\, is
  gemmed with stars which escape the naked eye\, but it sparkles like a bea
 d-spangled garment when viewed with the glass.\n— Garrett P. Serviss\, 
 “The Stars of Spring\,” from Astronomy with an Opera-Glass (1888)\, p.
  17\n\nToday’s image: Garrett P. Serviss\, Map 3 (Gemini\, Cancer and Ca
 nis Minor)\, from Astronomy with an Opera-Glass (1888)\, p. 18\n\nThe day 
 in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12
 -14
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-14
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0018@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261215
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:The Christmas Bird Count
DESCRIPTION:It is not many years ago that sportsmen were accustomed to meet
  on Christmas Day\, ‘choose sides\,’ and then\, as representatives of 
 the two bands resulting\, hie them to the fields and woods on the cheerful
  mission of killing practically everything in fur or feathers that crossed
  their path—if they could.\n\n. . . Now Bird-Lore proposes a new kind of
  Christmas side hunt\, in the form of a Christmas bird-census\, and we hop
 e that all our readers who have the opportunity will aid us in making it a
  success by spending a portion of Christmas Day with the birds and sending
  a report of their ‘hunt’ to Bird-Lore before they retire that night.\
 n— Frank M. Chapman\, “A Christmas Bird-Census”\, Bird-Lore 2 (Decem
 ber 1900)\, p. 192\n\nToday’s image: “The Christmas Bird Census\,” t
 he first returns\, from Bird-Lore 3 (January–February 1901)\, p. 29: New
  Brunswick\, Toronto\, Keene\, Belmont and Cambridge\, and the Arnold Arbo
 retum\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/se
 asons.html?day=12-15
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-15
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0017@earthlyhours.com
