RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Sea Mosses
DESCRIPTION:You will be surprised\, also\, to see what quantities of things
  you can find as late as November and December. Indeed\, if you are to kno
 w these plants thoroughly\, you must collect them at all seasons of the ye
 ar. Then you will know when they come\, and when they go\, and when they a
 re in their greatest perfection.\n— A. B. Hervey\, “Times and Places f
 or Collecting\,” from Sea Mosses: A Collector’s Guide and an Introduct
 ion to the Study of Marine Algae (1881)\, p. 14\n\nToday’s image: Loment
 aria baileyana and Nitophyllum andersonii\, plate XI from A. B. Hervey\, S
 ea Mosses: A Collector’s Guide (1881)\, colour plate\n\nThe day in The E
 arthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-16
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-16
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0016@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261217
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:American Holly
DESCRIPTION:I am unable to mark the northern limit of this tree with as muc
 h precision as that of many others\; but I believe it does not extend far 
 beyond Long Island\, though it is already common in Lower Jersey.\n— Fra
 nçois André Michaux\, “American Holly\,” from The North American Syl
 va\, vol. 2 (1819)\, p. 156\n\nToday’s image: Henri-Joseph Redouté (dra
 wing) and Renard (engraving)\, American Holly\, plate 84 from François An
 dré Michaux\, The North American Sylva\, vol. 2 (1819)\, colour-printed s
 tipple engraving\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyh
 ours.com/seasons.html?day=12-17
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-17
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0021@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261218
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:The Sun Standing Still
DESCRIPTION:Farmers\, friends\, companions of the sod\, we will not be somb
 rous and down-hearted\, because\, forsooth\, it may hap that we now close 
 the gates upon our pathway\, and no more\, after this\, walk together in o
 ur pleasant companionship. . . . We have ploughed\, we have sowed\, we hav
 e reapt\, we have mowed\; we have enjoyed the fruits of our industry\, and
  relished the good things of life in health and thankfulness. . . . Let th
 ere be nothing morose and chilly in parting.\n— Robert B. Thomas\, “Fa
 rmer’s Calendar\,” December\, from The Farmer’s Almanack for 1842 (1
 841)\, p. 29\n\nToday’s image: December\, from The Farmer’s Almanack f
 or 1842\, calculated for Boston (1841)\, p. 29: a woodcut of Capricorn\, a
  verse\, the day-by-day weather and the Farmer’s Calendar\n\nThe day in 
 The Earthly Book of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-18
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-18
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dy-0022@earthlyhours.com
DTSTAMP:20261112T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261220
RRULE:FREQ=YEARLY
SUMMARY:Solstice Eve
DESCRIPTION:The sun\, this December morning\, has come to the end of his so
 uthern journey\, he climbs the whitish sky to the south over the white fur
 y of the Orleans shoals\, and takes on a silvery quality from the pallor o
 f the sky. On such a morning went ancient peoples to their hills\, and cri
 ed to the pale god to return to their woods and fields . . .\n— Henry Be
 ston\, “Midwinter\,” from The Outermost House (1928)\, pp. 62–63\n\n
 Today’s image: Bradford\, Sunrise from Cape Cod\, plate from Henry Besto
 n\, The Outermost House (1928)\, photograph\n\nThe day in The Earthly Book
  of Hours: https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-20
URL:https://earthlyhours.com/seasons.html?day=12-20
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
